Schedule Deferred User Tasks
at jobs run silently in the background
atd = at deamon
sshd = ssh daemon
httpd = http daemon
daemon (pronounced "dee-mon") is a background process that runs continuously and 
silently without any direct user interaction.
-------example at timespec----------------
at (TIMESPEC) 
at now +5min 
teatime tomorrow (teatime is 16:00)
noon +4 days
5pm august 3 2021
----------example at timespec------------
Inspect and Manage Defferred User Jobs
atq = used to list delayed user jobs queued up to run later via the at command.
-----------------------------------------
Remove Jobs from Schedule
atrm (JOBNUMBER)
----------------------------example using at-------------------------------
echo "date >> /home/student/myjob.txt" | at now +2min
atq = watch the queue
watch atq = to watch queue in real time
The command updates the output of the atq command every two seconds.
After the atd daemon removes the deferred job from the queue, press Ctrl+c to exit.
at -q g teatime
-q = is the queue flag
teatime = exactly at 16:00
at -q b 16:05
a = standard queue
b = batch jobs
A batch job is a task scheduled to run in the background only when the computer isn't busy.
By default, a batch job will only start when the system load average drops below 1.5.
c-z = have lower cpu priority
----------------------------example using at-------------------------------
---------------Describe Recurring User Jobs----------------------
Recurring jobs are scheduled to run repeatedly
The crond daemon reads multiple configuration files: one per user, and a set of system-wide files. 
-----------------Example crontab Command----------------
crontab -l	= List the jobs for the current user.
crontab -r	= Remove all jobs for the current user.
crontab -e	= Edit jobs for the current user.
crontab filename	= Remove all jobs, and replace them with jobs that are read from filename. 
This command uses stdin input when no file is specified.
